---
// WorldBar's privacy policy: the Privacy Policy URL for the App Store listing.
// It describes only what the app does. Keep it in step with the app if that changes.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const EMAIL = 'njvanderbyl@gmail.com';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy · WorldBar"
  description="WorldBar collects no personal data, has no accounts and makes no network requests. Your cities and preferences stay on your Mac."
  image="/og/worldbar.jpg"
  imageAlt="WorldBar: every timezone, one glance"
>
  <article class="doc shell">
    <p class="doc__crumb t-annotation"><a href={`${base}worldbar/`}>WorldBar</a> · Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="doc__title">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="doc__meta t-annotation">Effective 17 November 2026 · Applies to the WorldBar app for Mac</p>

    <div class="doc__panel">
      <p>
        <strong>The short version.</strong> WorldBar has no accounts and no servers of its own. It does not collect,
        send or share any personal data, and it asks for no permissions. Your cities and settings stay on your Mac.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Who I am</h2>
    <p>
      WorldBar is made and published by an independent developer, Nathan VanderByl. Contact:
      <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}?subject=WorldBar%20privacy`}>{EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>2. What WorldBar does not do</h2>
    <p>
      It has no sign-in, no analytics, no advertising, no crash reporting and no tracking of any kind. The app runs
      in the macOS App Sandbox without network access, so it cannot send anything off your Mac. Time zone
      calculations and the list of cities are built into the app and work offline.
    </p>

    <h2>3. What is stored on your Mac</h2>
    <p>
      Your chosen cities and their order, your home city, your time format, appearance and menu bar choices, and your
      meeting preferences (duration, working hours, which cities take part). These are saved in the app's own
      preferences inside its sandbox container. Deleting the app's data removes them. They are never synced or uploaded.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Permissions</h2>
    <p>
      WorldBar does not request access to your calendar, contacts, location, files, photos, camera or microphone.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Creating a calendar event</h2>
    <p>
      When you click "Create calendar event…", WorldBar writes a small event file (the meeting's date and time, and
      the times in the cities you selected) to its temporary folder and opens it in the app you chose: Calendar or
      Outlook. You review the event there and decide whether to save it. WorldBar does not read your calendar and
      never creates an event on its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you choose "Outlook on the web" in Settings, WorldBar opens a Microsoft Outlook link in your default browser
      with the same meeting details in the address. That is the only case where meeting details leave your Mac, it
      happens only when you click, and Microsoft's own privacy policy then applies to what you do on their page.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Launch at login</h2>
    <p>
      If you turn this on, WorldBar uses the macOS login item system. That setting is managed by macOS and is
      visible in System Settings, General, Login Items.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Children</h2>
    <p>WorldBar collects no data from anyone, including children.</p>

    <h2>8. Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If WorldBar ever changes how it handles data, I will update this page and the effective date above before the
      change ships.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Questions</h2>
    <p>Email <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}?subject=WorldBar%20privacy`}>{EMAIL}</a>.</p>

    <p class="doc__links t-annotation">
      <a href={`${base}worldbar/`}>WorldBar</a> · <a href={`${base}worldbar/support/`}>Support</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  :global(html) {
    scroll-snap-type: none !important;
  }

  :global(.site-header) {
    position: static !important;
  }

  .doc {
    padding-block: 56px 112px;
    max-width: calc(820px + 2 * var(--pad));
  }

  .doc__crumb {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }

  .doc__crumb a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .doc__crumb a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .doc__title {
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 8vw, 5rem);
    line-height: 0.95;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  .doc__meta {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }

  .doc__panel {
    padding: 20px 22px;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-border);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-worldbar);
    border-radius: 14px;
    background:
      radial-gradient(80% 120% at 100% 0%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-worldbar) 14%, transparent), transparent 70%),
      var(--color-surface);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.15;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 36px 0 10px;
  }

  article p {
    max-width: 66ch;
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }

  .doc__links {
    margin-top: 48px;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
